faq:
  - q: "What equipment is used for cured in place pipe?"
    a: "A full lining crew carries inspection cameras, a hydro-jetter and cutting heads for cleaning, a wet-out table or pre-impregnated liner in refrigerated storage, an inversion drum or pull-in winch with a calibration bladder, a cure source (boiler, steam generator or UV light train), a robotic reinstatement cutter, and a locator. Everything rides on one or two trucks or a trailer."
  - q: "What is an inversion drum?"
    a: "A pressure vessel the wet liner is loaded into, with a nozzle at the outlet. Compressed air behind the liner pushes it out of the nozzle and turns it inside out down the pipe. Drums handle laterals and small mains; large mains are often inverted with a water column from a scaffold tower instead."
  - q: "What is a wet-out table?"
    a: "A long roller table where resin is drawn through the dry liner under vacuum and pressed to an even thickness between calibrated rollers. It produces a consistent liner and is the sign of a contractor who manufactures their own liner rather than buying it pre-saturated."
  - q: "Hot water, steam or UV: which cure is best?"
    a: "Each has a place. Hot water is forgiving and common on residential felt liners. Steam cures faster and uses less water. UV light cures glass-reinforced liners quickly with low odor and precise control, but the liner and light train cost more. The right choice depends on the liner system, the diameter and the site, not on one being universally better."
  - q: "What does a robotic cutter do?"
    a: "After the liner cures, a camera-guided cutter is driven into the pipe to reopen every branch connection the liner covered. The operator watches on a monitor and grinds a clean, round opening. The same tool, before lining, grinds down protruding joints and offsets so the liner lies flat."
  - q: "Does a plumber need all this to line a pipe?"
    a: "Yes, which is why most do not. The equipment, the trained crew and the manufacturer certification are a real barrier to entry. A company that owns this gear and uses it every week is a lining contractor; a company that rents it occasionally is learning on your sewer."
