<p class="cl-lead">Cured in place pipe equipment is the clearest tell of who you are hiring. A plumbing truck carries wrenches and a snake. A lining truck carries a wet-out table, an inversion drum, a boiler or a UV light train and a robotic cutter, and the people who know how to run them. Here is what each piece does, in the order it is used, and what its presence or absence tells you.</p>

<p>If you want the process the equipment serves, the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/installation">cured in place pipe installation</a> page walks through the day; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe">cured in place pipe</a> overview covers the method itself.</p>

<h2>1. Inspection: cameras and locators</h2>
<p>Every job starts and ends with a camera. For laterals it is a <strong>push camera</strong>, a self-leveling head on a stiff cable with a footage counter. For mains it is a <strong>crawler</strong>, a tracked robot with a pan-and-tilt head that the operator drives from a truck. Public specifications call for a color pan-and-tilt camera and a recording made within 24 hours before lining. A <strong>sonde locator</strong> picks up a transmitter in the camera head from the surface, so defects and branch connections can be marked on the ground. Recording and reporting software logs the footage marker at each observation; some crews code defects to the NASSCO PACP standard so engineers can read them.</p>

<h2>2. Cleaning: jetters and cutting heads</h2>
<p>The <strong>hydro-jetter</strong> is a high-pressure pump, often trailer-mounted, that drives a nozzle down the pipe on a hose; rear-facing jets pull it forward and scour the wall. For roots, a <strong>root cutter</strong> or <strong>chain flail</strong> on the jetter or on a cable machine chews through the mass. For cast iron scale, a <strong>descaling</strong> chain or carbide head is run in passes. <strong>Milling and grinding heads</strong> on the robotic cutter take down protruding joints and offsets. Debris goes downstream to a vacuum truck or a trap. Cleaning equipment does not look impressive, but the time a crew spends with it is the best single predictor of how long the liner lasts.</p>

<h2>3. Liner preparation: wet-out table or refrigerated transport</h2>
<p>There are two ways to get resin into a liner. A <strong>wet-out table</strong> is a long bench with a vacuum port and calibrated pinch rollers; resin is poured into the tube, air is drawn out under vacuum and the rollers squeeze the resin through the felt to a set thickness. Crews that wet out on site carry the table in a truck, along with resin drums, catalyst, mixing gear and scales. The alternative is <strong>factory-impregnated liner</strong>, saturated at a manufacturer's plant and shipped in <strong>refrigerated trucks</strong> or coolers so the resin does not start curing early. UV liners are always factory-impregnated and come in light-proof wrapping. Either way, a <strong>resin calculation</strong> for the specific pipe comes first, and a good crew can show it to you.</p>

<h2>4. Installation: inversion drums, standpipes and winches</h2>
<p>An <strong>inversion drum</strong> (also called a shooter or inverter) is a pressure vessel with a nozzle. The wet liner is coiled inside, the open end is cuffed over the nozzle, and compressed air pushes the liner out so it turns inside out and travels down the pipe with the resin side pressed to the wall. Drums come in sizes for 2-inch kitchen lines up to small mains. For large-diameter mains, crews often use a <strong>water inversion standpipe</strong> or scaffold tower instead, letting the weight of a water column drive the liner. For <strong>pull-in-place</strong> work, a <strong>winch</strong> at the far access point drags the liner into position and a <strong>calibration bladder</strong> is inverted inside it and inflated. Air compressors and pressure gauges hold the liner against the pipe for the whole cure.</p>

<h2>5. Curing: boilers, steam generators and UV light trains</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hot water boiler.</strong> A trailer or truck-mounted boiler heats water and a pump circulates it through the inflated liner. Thermocouples at the inlet, the outlet and between the liner and the host pipe at the far end feed a data logger, and the cure is run to the resin manufacturer's time-and-temperature schedule.</li>
  <li><strong>Steam generator.</strong> Produces steam that is fed into the liner with a mixing valve to control temperature. Faster than water, with far less to dispose of afterward; cool-down is controlled with air.</li>
  <li><strong>UV light train.</strong> For glass-reinforced liners under ASTM F2019: a chain of UV lamps on a carriage is pulled through the inflated liner at a computer-controlled speed, curing the resin as it goes. The system logs lamp output, speed and internal temperature along the length. Low odor, fast, and precise, at a higher cost for liner and gear.</li>
</ul>
<p>Whichever cure is used, look for the <strong>data logger</strong>. A printed or electronic cure log is the proof the liner reached temperature for the required time. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/liner-testing">cured in place pipe liner testing</a> page explains what the lab does with a sample afterward.</p>

<h2>6. Reinstatement: robotic cutters</h2>
<p>A <strong>robotic reinstatement cutter</strong> is a small tracked or skid-mounted machine with a camera and an air- or hydraulic-powered grinding head. The operator drives it to each connection the liner covered, finds the dimple or uses the pre-lining footage to locate it, and grinds an opening of 90 to 100 percent of the branch diameter with smooth edges. Lateral cutters for 4- and 6-inch pipe are compact; main-line cutters are larger and faster. A crew without a cutter either digs to reopen connections or lines only pipes without any, which limits what they can do.</p>

<h2>7. Support gear</h2>
<p>Generators and compressors, water tanks and a source hookup, hoses, end seals and cuffs, pressure and temperature instruments, bypass pumps when a live main has to keep flowing, and personal protective gear for resin handling. Respirators and ventilation fans come out when styrene-based resins are cured in or near occupied buildings. Traffic control shows up on anything in the street.</p>

<h2>What the equipment tells you</h2>
<p>You do not need to know the brand of inversion drum to judge a contractor. Ask three things: <em>Do you own your lining equipment?</em> <em>Do you wet out your own liner or use factory-impregnated, and which system?</em> <em>Do you have a reinstatement cutter?</em> Confident, specific answers are what you want. Every installer listed on this site has been asked those questions already; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/training-and-certifications">training and certifications</a> page covers the credentials that go with the gear, and <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/near-me">cured in place pipe near me</a> covers how to find the right crew for your area.</p>
